{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>Workspace Layout</h2>
          <p>
            Switch machines or toggle <code class="mono">mm</code>/<code class="mono">in</code> from the top bar anytime. The screen splits into three columns: the <strong>Left Sidebar</strong> (stock size, plasma settings, 3D Assembly Overview, and STEP upload/fixtures), the <strong>Center Workspace</strong> (3D Part Preview, inline DXF/SVG Tube Overlay controls, 2D Unwrapped Feature Map, and Feature Table), and the <strong>Right Terminal</strong> (live G-code). Cut-path and marking edits stay synced across Single-Part and Nesting views.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Left Panel</span>
              <span class="value">3D Assembly &amp; Plasma Params</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Center Workspace</span>
              <span class="value">Part Inspection &amp; 3D Simulation</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Right Terminal</span>
              <span class="value">Live XYZA G-Code Post-Processor</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Load the <code class="mono">Saddle Joint (Zero-Bevel)</code> CAD Fixture</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Drag any single-tube or multi-body <code class="mono">.step</code> / <code class="mono">.stp</code> assembly onto the <strong>CAD Geometry</strong> dropzone in the left sidebar. To test a saddle right away without uploading your own file, use <strong>Quick CAD Fixtures</strong> right below it:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Find <span class="ui-chip cyan">Quick CAD Fixtures</span> in the left sidebar.</li>
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip">Saddle Joint (Zero-Bevel)</span> (<code class="mono">DUAL-CURVE</code>) to load a two-tube T-joint where a branch pipe (<code class="mono">OD 27.0 mm × 3.0 mm wall</code>) copes onto a main chord tube.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1: Clicking 'Saddle Joint (Zero-Bevel)' in the Quick CAD Fixtures panel</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-1-fixture.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-1-fixture.png" alt="Step 1: Quick CAD Fixtures panel highlighting Saddle Joint (Zero-Bevel)" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Pick Your Active Tube in the Model Overview or Solid Bar</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Multi-body STEP files split into separate tubes automatically, each tagged with its cross-section (<code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Round</code>, <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect</code>, etc.) plus inner and outer walls:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Entire Model Overview (Top-Left 3D View):</strong> Shows the full assembly. Your active tube glows <strong>amber/orange</strong> (<code class="mono">Part 2: solid_2</code>) while other tubes stay steel gray. <strong>Click any tube in 3D</strong> to switch straight to it.</li>
              <li><strong>Active CAD Solid Bar (Top of Center Panel):</strong> Shows your active part's size (<code class="mono">Part 2: solid_2 (OD 27.0mm • L=54.8mm)</code>). You can also step through tubes with the dropdown or <span class="ui-chip cyan">◀</span> / <span class="ui-chip cyan">▶</span> arrows.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2: Selecting parts interactively in the Entire Model Overview or Active CAD Solid bar</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-2-overview.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-2-overview.png" alt="Step 2: Entire Model Overview 3D assembly viewer and Active CAD Solid selector bar" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Check the 3D Part Preview &amp; Cut Paths</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Below the solid bar, <strong>3D Part Preview &amp; Cutting Contours</strong> shows your tube and its color-coded torch path:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong style="color: #f87171;">● Red (Lead-In):</strong> Pierce point and entry sweep from scrap onto the cut line.</li>
              <li><strong style="color: #38bdf8;">● Cyan (Cut Contour):</strong> Kerf-compensated 3D path around the tube.</li>
              <li><strong style="color: #4ade80;">● Green (Lead-Out):</strong> Overburn or circular exit at the end of the cut.</li>
              <li><strong>Click-to-Move Pierces &amp; Tabs (<code class="mono">v0.8-C</code>):</strong> Click anywhere on the 3D part or 2D unwrapped map to move a pierce point (<span class="ui-chip">Move Pierce</span>, <code class="mono">Maker</code> &amp; <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>), or click, drag, and delete holding tabs (<span class="ui-chip">Edit Tabs</span>, <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>). Lead-ins and lead-outs update automatically in both Single-File and Pack Mode.</li>
              <li>Orbit, pan, and zoom with your mouse, or snap views with <code class="mono">Iso</code>, <code class="mono">Side</code>, <code class="mono">End</code>, and <code class="mono">Top</code> in the top-right corner.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 3: 3D Part Preview showing color-coded Lead-In (Red), Cut Contour (Cyan), and Lead-Out (Green)</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-3-preview.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-3-preview.png" alt="Step 3: 3D Part Preview and Cutting Contours viewport" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Select the <code class="mono">ENDCUT</code> Row in the Feature Table</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Under the 3D preview and the expandable <strong>2D Unwrapped Stock Feature Map</strong>, the feature table lists every cut on your tube: <span class="ui-chip emerald">STARTCUT</span> (chuck end), <span class="ui-chip emerald">ENDCUT</span> (free-end saddle or miter), and <span class="ui-chip cyan">CUTOUT</span> (holes, slots, and copes).
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click the <span class="ui-chip">ENDCUT</span> row (<code class="mono">7E99200A-EBB2-4D59-9B57-AB5D515E73D3</code> at <code class="mono">X = 48.3 mm, A = 267.5°</code>) to highlight that saddle in the 3D viewport.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4: Clicking the ENDCUT row to highlight the saddle joint contour in the 3D preview</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-4-select-endcut.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-4-select-endcut.png" alt="Step 4: Selecting the ENDCUT feature row in the feature table" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Click <code class="mono">⚙ Config</code> to Tune Lead-Ins, Holes &amp; Bevels</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Each row has inline dropdowns for Lead-In, Lead-Out, and Zero-Bevel. Click <span class="ui-chip">⚙ Config</span> in the right-hand <strong>ACTION</strong> column to open the full 4-column settings drawer for that cut:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Lead-In &amp; ERW Weld-Seam Avoidance:</strong> Pick <code class="mono">Rotational Arc</code>, <code class="mono">Tangent Arc</code>, or <code class="mono">Linear</code>. Set sweep length (<code class="mono">mm</code>) and scrap clearance (<code class="mono">mm</code>), or turn on <strong>3D Ramp Lead-In (Flying Pierce)</strong> to cut spatter. Pierces also avoid your tube's <strong>ERW mill weld seam</strong> on round pipe and square/rectangular HSS so you pierce clean steel instead of internal flash.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Lead-Out &amp; Small-Hole (<code class="mono">60%</code>) Speed:</strong> Set overburn (<code class="mono">mm</code>) or turn on <strong>Zero-Overburn Early Torch-Out</strong> (synced <code class="mono">M63</code> shutoff before contour end to prevent exit divots). Enclosed bolt holes (<code class="mono">&le; 25.4 mm</code> / <code class="mono">1&Prime;</code>) on round and rectangular tubes drop to <code class="mono">60%</code> cut speed automatically. Bolts drop straight through without reaming.</li>
              <li><strong>3. Corner Loop, Zero-Bevel &amp; 4-Axis Weld Bevel:</strong> Add rollout corner loops or tune <strong>Zero-Bevel Compensation</strong> (dual-curve pipe saddles and heel-only pullback on HSS miters). You can also enable <strong>4-Axis Round-Tube Eccentric Weld-Prep Beveling</strong> (<code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>, with 3D preview unlocked free on Beta &amp; Maker).</li>
              <li><strong>4. Kerf-Compensated Holding Tabs &amp; Smart Defaults:</strong> Leave uncut micro-bridges around a cut to hold slugs or parts in place. Drag or delete them directly on the canvas with <span class="ui-chip">Edit Tabs</span> (<code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>). Full kerf width is added to every tab gap so thin-wall tabs stay intact, and stock-aware defaults fill lead-in/out, corner loops, tabs, and zero-bevel settings until you override them.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 5: Expanding the per-contour Lead-In, Lead-Out, Corner Loop &amp; Zero-Bevel drawer</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-5-open-config.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-5-open-config.png" alt="Step 5: Clicking Config on the ENDCUT row to open the 4-column contour configuration drawer" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 6
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-6">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 06</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Enable Zero-Bevel Compensation (<code class="mono">80%</code>), Save, and Open 3D Simulation</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              On a 4-axis cutter, your torch stays perpendicular to the tube (no 5-axis tilting head). Cut a thick pipe saddle strictly along the outside CAD line and the inside wall will stick out at the flanks, holding the crown open with an ugly weld gap.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>In <strong>Corner Loop &amp; Zero-Bevel</strong>, check <span class="ui-chip">✓ Apply Zero-Bevel Compensation</span> and set <strong>Intensity (%)</strong> to <code class="mono">80%</code> (with <code class="mono">Cope / Fishmouth</code> checked).</li>
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Save</span> on the <code class="mono">ENDCUT</code> row. It turns <span class="ui-chip emerald">Saved ✓</span> and updates your toolpath immediately.</li>
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip">⚡ 3D Cut Simulation</span> in the top bar to watch the compensated 4-axis cut.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout info">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">🔬 How Zero-Bevel Works on Pipe Saddles &amp; HSS Miters</div>
              <p>
                <strong>Round Pipe Saddles &amp; Copes:</strong> Reading both your outer and inner CAD wall curves pulls the torch back to the inner wall at the flanks so your saddle drops onto the header pipe with zero gap.
              </p>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Square &amp; Rectangular HSS Miters &amp; Copes:</strong> An angled miter on flat-walled HSS only binds on the obtuse inside heel wall. Zero-Bevel pulls back <strong>only that interfering heel wall</strong> (<code class="mono">t &middot; tan(&theta;)</code>) and leaves your side walls and acute toes dead flush for zero-grinder fit-up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" style="margin-top: 1.25rem; border-left-color: var(--plasma-amber);">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">🔥 4-Axis Round-Tube Eccentric Weld-Prep Beveling</div>
              <p>
                For V-groove weld prep on round pipe without a 5-axis tilting head, turn on <strong>Eccentric Weld-Prep Beveling</strong>. Your torch shifts off-center in <code class="mono">Y</code>, drops <code class="mono">Z</code> to hold standoff, and counter-rotates <code class="mono">A</code> across all four quadrants using the pipe's natural slope. 3D preview is unlocked on <strong>Public Beta</strong> and <strong>Maker</strong>; G-code export is in <a href="../index.html#pricing" style="color: var(--amber-400); font-weight: 600;">Pro Shop ($399) &rarr;</a>
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">🛠️ Bent Tubing: 3D Cut-and-Bend Unfolding</div>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Drop a bent round pipe or square/rectangular HSS model to unfold it onto straight stock with mitered relief slots, dead-straight HSS cheek cuts, inner-wall clearance, and an uncut spine. <a href="bending.html" style="color: var(--amber-400); font-weight: 600;">Read Tutorial 04: Bending HSS &amp; HSS-O Sticks →</a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 6: Setting Zero-Bevel Intensity to 80%, saving the contour, and switching to 3D Cut Simulation</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-6-zero-bevel.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-6-zero-bevel.png" alt="Step 6: Enabling Apply Zero-Bevel Compensation at 80%, clicking Save, and selecting 3D Cut Simulation" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 7
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-7">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 07</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Run the Interactive 4-Axis 3D Cut Simulation</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              The <strong>3D Machine Simulation</strong> reads your actual right-hand G-code (<code class="mono">PROGRAM: G-CODE</code>) and plays back the 4-axis motion in real time:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip">▶ Play</span> in the bottom bar. Scrub the timeline, jump <code class="mono">-1s</code> / <code class="mono">+1s</code>, toggle <code class="mono">⚡ 10x Rapids</code>, or change speed from <code class="mono">0.25x</code> to <code class="mono">8x</code>.</li>
              <li>Watch the <strong>Live CNC DRO (<code class="mono">XYZA</code>)</strong> in the top-left corner track <code class="mono">X, Y, Z</code> (mm), <code class="mono">A</code> (degrees), and live feedrate as your tube spins under the torch.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 7: Clicking Play in the 3D Cut Simulation with live 4-axis XYZA DRO</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-7-sim-start.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-7-sim-start.png" alt="Step 7: 3D Cut Simulation showing Play button and live CNC DRO (XYZA)" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 8
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-8">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 08</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Verify the Completed 4-Axis Toolpath &amp; Export G-Code</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              At <code class="mono">100%</code> (<span class="ui-chip emerald">FINISHED</span>), the full 3D path (rapids, arc lead-ins, kerf- and zero-bevel-compensated cuts, and lead-outs) wraps cleanly around the tube:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Check the right-hand <strong>G-Code Post-Processor Terminal</strong> to verify header notes, stock size, synced <code class="mono">M63/M62</code> THC locks, and <code class="mono">G1 X... Y... Z... A... F...</code> blocks.</li>
              <li>Click the download button in the terminal header to save your <code class="mono">.nc</code> file.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 8: Simulation completed at 100% showing the verified 4-axis saddle cut</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/cut-path-8-sim-done.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/cut-path-8-sim-done.png" alt="Step 8: Completed 3D Cut Simulation at 100% showing full 4-axis toolpath" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
